    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the period of the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\), we start by recalling the periodic properties of the tangent and cotangent functions. The tangent and cotangent functions are reciprocal and periodic, with periods that are related by their functions' definitions.\n\nFor any integer \\( n \\),\n\\[\n\\tan(x + p \\pi) = \\tan(x), \\tan \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} - x \\right) = \\cot(x),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cot(x + p \\pi) = \\cot(x).\n\\]\n\nExpressing \\( y \\) in a simple analytical form, we need \\( \\cot x \\), calculate multiple angles and integrate to get them back together.\n\nUpdated solution:\n\nTo determine the period of \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\), utilizing the fact that \\(\\tan\\left(x + \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = -\\cot x\\) and exploiting the properties of trigonometric periodical function.\n\nThe graph of \\(\\cot(x)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\ny &= \\tan{1+\\pi \\cdot n}\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nGiven simplification:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\ny &= \\cot{(1+\\pi \\cdot n)} + \\tan{(1+\\pi \\cdot n)} \\\\\n&~~~~~~~~~~+ \\cot{(1+\\pi \\cdot n)} + \\tan{(1+\\pi \\cdot n)} \\\\\n&=\\dots + \\tan{(1+\\pi \\cdot n)} \\\\\n&=\\dots + \\tan{(1+\\pi \\cdot n)} \\\\\n&=\\dots + 0 \\\\\n&= \\text{period}\\left( y \\right)\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nTherefore:\n\n\\[\n\\text{The period of } y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\text{ is } 2\\pi.\n\\]\n\nSo the period of \\(y = \\tan x + \\cot x\\) is \\(\\boxed{2\\pi}\\).",
